<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Kitashirakawa Yoshihisa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rinnōji-no-miya Yoshihisa, Kitashirakawa Yoshihisa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Kitashirakawa Yoshihisa was a member of the Japanese imperial family who, as abbot of the Rinnōji temple at Ueno, fled Edo in 1868 and became nominal head of the Ōuetsu Reppan Dōmei, the northern alliance resisting the new imperial government. Pardoned after its defeat, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and became an army officer. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1895 while commanding troops in the Japanese invasion of the island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-kitashirakawa-yoshihisa.jpg" alt="Prince Kitashirakawa Yoshihisa" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Kitashirakawa_Yoshihisa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
